  "ablation_size_label": "size = 16384",
  "takeaways": [
    "**Blackwell (`B200`) vs. Hopper/Ampere/Ada (`H100`, `A100`, `L4`):**\n- On **B200** (`148 SMs`, `128 MB L2`, `8 TB/s HBM3e`), doubling per-thread work to **`2x half4` (`16 B/thread`) + in-place read-modify-write (`A += B`)** cut runtime from `276.08 µs` (#59) to **`233.48 µs` public / `232.89 µs` secret (#20 / 66, `6.90 TB/s`)** — a **15.4% speedup** (`96.6%` of the `#1` entry on the leaderboard).\n- On **H100**, **A100**, and **L4**, the smaller L2 caches (`40–50 MB`) and lower DRAM bandwidth mean `16384×16384` (`1.61 GB`) is strictly DRAM-bandwidth-bound. The minimal **10-register `direct_half4`** kernel (`1x half4` per thread, out-of-place) maximizes warp occupancy and achieves **`526.44 µs` (#24 on H100, `97.4%` of #1)**, **`912.90 µs` (#54 on A100, `96.8%` of #1)**, and **`6,891.89 µs` (#21 on L4, `97.6%` of #1)**.",
    "**Grid-Stride Loops vs. Direct 1-to-1 Indexing on Single-Pass Elementwise Kernels:**\n- Replacing `loop-vector-stride` (`557.86 µs` on H100) with direct global indexing (`526.44 µs` on H100) saved `31.4 µs` by eliminating `cudaOccupancyMaxActiveBlocksPerMultiprocessor` host queries and loop-branch bookkeeping, dropping register pressure from `20` to `10` registers per thread."
  ],
